From 2e172535df951817412cf39c6b35647a3c2588b2 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Ryan Brue <ryanbrue.dev@gmail.com>
Date: Thu, 3 Sep 2026 14:08:03 -0500
Subject: [PATCH] power: supply: add a fuel gauge driver for the MediaTek
 MT6397 PMIC

The MT6397 contains a fuel gauge (FGADC) that measures battery current
across a board-provided sense resistor and integrates it in hardware into a
charge accumulation register (CAR). This driver runs the same battery-meter
algorithm MediaTek's downstream kernels run on top of that hardware
(drivers/power/mt81xx, CONFIG_SOC_BY_HW_FG), so that a board ported from
such a kernel reports the same state of charge it did before:

 - At boot the state of charge is seeded from the open-circuit voltage the
   PMIC latched by itself at power-on, cross-checked against the live
   voltage compensated for I*R, and against the value the previous boot
   left in an RTC spare register.
 - From then on it is coulomb counted: DOD1 = DOD0 - CAR / Qmax, where
   Qmax is interpolated from per-temperature capacities and reduced for
   the load actually drawn.
 - The OCV, internal-resistance and Qmax tables are all per temperature and
   re-interpolated as the pack temperature (from an NTC on the PMIC's
   auxiliary ADC) moves.
 - A charger reporting full re-anchors the counter at 100 %, and the
   displayed percentage moves towards the computed one by at most one point
   per step, so it never jumps. It sits at 99 % until the charger says full,
   ramps to 100 % over the vendor's 60 s steps once it does, and holds
   100 % through the charger's recharge cycles while the cable stays in.

Every register, scaling constant and table comes from the vendor's own
meter (mt6397_fgadc.c, mt_battery_meter.c) or the battery vendor's
characterisation in the board's device tree; nothing here is measured or
estimated by this driver. Two details are easy to get wrong and are worth
recording: the 36-bit accumulator is read as the vendor does, a 16-bit
window of bits 29:14 taken from CON3[15:14] and CON2[13:0] with the sign
in CON1 bit 3 and an all-ones window meaning zero; and the hardware OCV is
read from the register the power-path (SWCHR) build of the vendor uses,
ADC20, not the non-power-path one.

The state of charge is persisted in bits 14:8 of the RTC alarm-hour
register, which the alarm never uses; the vendor's RTC does the same, and
rtc-mt6397 masks the hour to five bits so the field survives an alarm being
set. The driver issues the RTC's write trigger itself after the write.

Tested on the Amazon Fire HD 10 (2017) with the Desay pack's tables.

Signed-off-by: Ryan Brue <ryanbrue.dev@gmail.com>
---
 drivers/power/supply/Kconfig          |   12 +
 drivers/power/supply/Makefile         |    1 +
 drivers/power/supply/mt6397-battery.c | 1405 +++++++++++++++++++++++++
 3 files changed, 1418 insertions(+)
 create mode 100644 drivers/power/supply/mt6397-battery.c

